# HTML Forms and Input Elements

## 1. What is an HTML Form?

An HTML form is used to collect information from users and send it for processing.

Forms are commonly used for:
- Login pages
- Registration pages
- Contact forms
- Feedback forms
- Search boxes

The `<form>` element is used to create a form in HTML.

## 2. Basic Form Structure

```html
<form>
    <label for="username">Username:</label>
    <input type="text" id="username">

    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>
```

### Explanation

- `<form>` creates the form.
- `<label>` provides a description for an input.
- `<input>` allows users to enter information.
- `<button>` creates a clickable button.
- `type="submit"` submits the form.

## 3. Common Input Types

The `type` attribute defines what kind of information an input field accepts.

### Text Input

Used to enter normal text.

```html
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter your name">
```

### Password Input

Used to enter passwords. Characters are hidden visually.

```html
<input type="password" placeholder="Enter password">
```

### Email Input

Used to enter an email address.

```html
<input type="email" placeholder="Enter your email">
```

### Number Input

Used to enter numerical values.

```html
<input type="number" placeholder="Enter your age">
```

### Date Input

Allows users to select a date.

```html
<input type="date">
```

### Radio Button

Allows users to select one option from a group.

```html
<input type="radio" name="gender" value="male"> Male
<input type="radio" name="gender" value="female"> Female
```

Radio buttons in the same group use the same `name` attribute.

### Checkbox

Allows users to select one or more options.

```html
<input type="checkbox" name="skill" value="html"> HTML
<input type="checkbox" name="skill" value="css"> CSS
```

### Submit Button

Used to submit the form.

```html
<input type="submit" value="Submit">
```

## 4. Important Form Attributes

### `action`

Specifies where the form data should be sent.

```html
<form action="/submit">
```

### `method`

Defines how form data is sent.

Two common methods are:

- `GET` – sends form data in the URL.
- `POST` – sends form data in the request body.

```html
<form action="/submit" method="POST">
```

### `placeholder`

Displays a short hint inside an input field.

```html
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter your name">
```

### `required`

Makes an input field mandatory.

```html
<input type="email" required>
```

### `name`

Identifies the input field when form data is submitted.

```html
<input type="text" name="username">
```

## 5. Complete Registration Form Example

### HTML

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Registration Form</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h2>Registration Form</h2>

    <form action="/register" method="POST">

        <label for="name">Full Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" name="name" required>

        <br><br>

        <label for="email">Email:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required>

        <br><br>

        <label for="password">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" id="password" name="password" required>

        <br><br>

        <label for="dob">Date of Birth:</label>
        <input type="date" id="dob" name="dob">

        <br><br>

        <label>
            <input type="checkbox" name="terms" required>
            I agree to the terms and conditions.
        </label>

        <br><br>

        <button type="submit">Register</button>

    </form>

</body>
</html>
```

Note: `/register` is an example server endpoint. A working registration form needs a backend to receive and process the submitted information.

## 6. Difference Between GET and POST

| GET | POST |
|---|---|
| Sends data through the URL | Sends data in the request body |
| Data may appear in browser history | Data does not normally appear in the URL |
| Useful for searches and retrieving data | Commonly used for submitting information |
| Should not be used for sensitive information | Requires HTTPS and secure backend handling for sensitive information |

